(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-external-middle-ear-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"External and Middle Ear Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the treatment of fungal external ear infection?","choices":["Debris removal and topical antifungals","Oral antifungals alone","Antipseudomonal antibiotics","Surgical canal reconstruction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — cleaning matters as much as the drops. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which instrument is used to remove a soft foreign body from the ear canal?","choices":["A curette","Alligator forceps","A right-angle hook","A bulb syringe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; firm objects are taken with a loop or hook instead. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which otoscopic finding fits otitis media with effusion?","choices":["A normal pearly grey drum","Fungal filaments across the canal","A dull drum with an air and fluid level","A bright red bulging drum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with reduced mobility on insufflation. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What distinguishes otitis media with effusion from acute otitis media?","choices":["It always follows trauma","There is fluid but no acute infection","There is infection but no fluid","The tympanic membrane is always perforated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which additional procedure is required when a cholesteatoma extends into the mastoid with bony erosion?","choices":["Mastoidectomy","Stapedectomy","Adenoidectomy","Myringotomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the characteristic otoscopic finding in eustachian tube dysfunction?","choices":["A bulging tympanic membrane","A perforated tympanic membrane","Blood behind the tympanic membrane","A retracted tympanic membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Negative middle ear pressure pulls the drum inward. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which symptom is characteristic of otitis externa?","choices":["Sudden vertigo","Pain worsened by moving the auricle or tragus","Pain relieved by moving the auricle","Painless bloody discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the single most useful bedside sign. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is the function of the eustachian tube?","choices":["Sensing head position","Draining tears","Equalising middle ear pressure","Transmitting sound to the cochlea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It allows air to pass and keeps pressure balanced across the drum. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which triad of symptoms characterises acute otitis media?","choices":["Itching, discharge and canal tenderness","Facial weakness, severe pain and discharge","Otalgia, fever and hearing loss","Vertigo, tinnitus and hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the consequence of failing to drain an auricular haematoma early?","choices":["Progression to cholesteatoma","Cauliflower ear","Chronic vertigo","Facial paralysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — permanent cartilage deformity. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which pair of medications is used for eustachian tube dysfunction?","choices":["Antihistamines and oral corticosteroids","Diuretics and a low-salt diet","Decongestants and intranasal corticosteroids","Topical antifungals and debridement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both reduce the mucosal oedema obstructing the tube. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the expected course of most episodes of acute otitis media?","choices":["Progression to cholesteatoma","Chronic suppuration in most cases","Spontaneous resolution","Progression to mastoiditis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why watchful waiting is reasonable. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which examination findings accompany carcinoma of the ear canal?","choices":["A retracted but intact drum","Bilateral symmetrical bony growths","A normal canal and drum","A friable canal with bloody discharge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with an abnormal growth visible. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What should be done with tissue avulsed from the auricle?","choices":["Bring it for reattachment","Discard it as non-viable","Freeze it for later grafting","Submit it for pathology only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reattachment is often successful. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"When is observation alone appropriate in otosclerosis?","choices":["When the patient is under thirty","When the loss is bilateral and severe","When tinnitus is present","When it is unilateral or the patient is untroubled"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What does insufflation during otoscopy assess?","choices":["Mobility of the tympanic membrane","Colour of the tympanic membrane","Hearing threshold","Facial nerve function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A drum that will not move signals pressure or fluid behind it. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Why must irrigation be avoided when an organic foreign body is in the canal?","choices":["Organic material swells when wet","Irrigation always perforates the drum","Organic material becomes sharp when wet","Water causes chemical burns"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which lodges it harder. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which subtype of chronic otitis media has a dry perforation with no active infection?","choices":["Adhesive","Benign","With effusion","Necrotizing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which procedure addresses adenoid hypertrophy obstructing the eustachian tubes?","choices":["Tonsillectomy","Mastoidectomy","Myringoplasty","Adenoidectomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is mastoiditis?","choices":["A keratin collection in the middle ear","A vascular tumour of the middle ear","Infection spreading into the mastoid air cells","Infection of the external canal skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, as a complication of acute otitis media. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which intervention is used for refractory or recurrent acute otitis media?","choices":["Ear canal reconstruction","Tympanostomy tubes","Mastoidectomy","Cochlear implantation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, placed by an ear, nose and throat specialist. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which otoscopic finding indicates bleeding into the middle ear after barotrauma?","choices":["Canal exostoses","A chalky white mass","Haemotympanum","A retraction pocket"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — blood visible behind the drum. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which description fits normal cerumen?","choices":["A protective, thick, oily secretion","A watery, clear secretion","A dry keratin flake from the drum","A blood-stained fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is protective rather than merely waste. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Alongside mastoiditis, which of the following is a recognised complication of acute otitis media?","choices":["Otosclerosis","Presbycusis","Cerumen impaction","Tympanic membrane perforation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis and rarely meningitis also appear on that list. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient with tympanostomy tubes needs wax removed. Who should do it?","choices":["A pharmacist with over-the-counter drops","An ear, nose and throat specialist","Any clinician using irrigation","The patient, using cotton swabs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tubes and perforations both move removal to a specialist. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which organism causes the largest share of otitis externa?","choices":["Aspergillus niger","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, at around thirty-eight per cent. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which history point most changes the organisms to worry about in a child with acute otitis media?","choices":["Immunisation status","Number of siblings","Season of the year","Whether the child was breastfed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An unimmunised child puts Haemophilus influenzae back on the list. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In which part of the external canal are the glands that produce cerumen found?","choices":["Throughout the whole canal","The middle ear","The mastoid air cells","The outer third"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cartilaginous outer third carries the sebaceous and ceruminous glands. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In which space does blood collect in an auricular haematoma?","choices":["The middle ear","The external canal","The mastoid air cells","The sub-perichondrial space"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is what separates cartilage from its blood supply. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which structure is the small projection lying immediately in front of the ear canal opening?","choices":["Tragus","Helix","Antihelix","Concha"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It sits anterior to the canal opening, which is why pressing it hurts in canal infection. — Disorders External and Middle Ear 9-2026.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
